    rule1Title: "1. Hydrodynamic Blade Lift-Off & Boundary Layer Renewal",
    rule1Text: "As the mutator shaft rotates inside a cylindrical heat exchanger, viscous fluid creates a hydrodynamic lubrication wedge beneath the scraper blade edge. If the radial hydrodynamic lift force exceeds blade spring pre-load and centrifugal force, the blade lifts away from the cylinder wall (film gap h > 20 μm). This leaves an un-scraped stagnant boundary layer that rapidly overheats, causing instant protein burn-on fouling, caramelization, and severe heat transfer collapse. Ensure spring pre-load is sized to maintain continuous contact (h < 5 μm) across all operating viscosity ranges.",
    rule2Title: "2. Protein Thermal Denaturation & Biot Number Fouling Limits",
    rule2Text: "In aseptic processing of proteinaceous fluids (infant formula, milk, egg products), wall temperatures exceeding 75°C induce rapid unfolding and cross-linking of beta-lactoglobulin and casein micelles. If the boundary layer Biot number (Bi = h·L / k) exceeds 0.1, internal thermal resistance dominates, creating steep temperature gradients where wall-contacting product scorches before bulk fluid reaches sterilization targets. Maintain scraping frequencies above 15 Hz to strip thermal boundary layers before fouling crystallization can nucleate.",
    rule3Title: "3. Blade Chattering Resonant Cavitation & Liner Micro-Gouging",
    rule3Text: "Shaft misalignment or excessive radial runout (>0.4 mm) forces scraper blades to oscillate rapidly across varying wall clearances. At elevated mutator speeds, this cyclic impact triggers acoustic blade chattering (vibration levels exceeding 4.5 g RMS). Chattering induces localized acoustic cavitation in the fluid wedge and hammers the cylinder liner, producing microscopic gouging and chrome plating delamination. Delaminated hard chrome flakes (850 HV) contaminate product streams and destroy sanitary cleanability.",
    rule4Title: "4. 3-A Sanitary Standard 31-07 & Aseptic Steam Barrier Seals",
    rule4Text: "Under 3-A Sanitary Standard 31-07, all food contact surfaces must achieve Ra ≤ 0.8 μm finish and permit complete CIP cleaning and steam sterilization. Scraper blades must be certified FDA food contact polymers (e.g. virgin PEEK 21 CFR § 177.2415) and inspected for micro-shaving fractures every 500 operating hours. Mutator drive shafts must feature dual mechanical seals with a continuous sterile steam barrier condensate flush (1.0 to 1.5 bar) per FDA 21 CFR § 113.40 to prevent atmospheric bacterial and spore ingress.",
